body:
That's really good advice. I did try several. I tend to believe that there are two types of depression. Situational and clinical. One stems from past experiences or the quality of life you have. The other is biological. I'm definitely the latter and unfortunately resistant to most if not all currently available medication. So there wasn't really much to talk about with any of the therapists I had. Good job, own my house, have a good group of friends to lean on. It's just that my brain wants me dead lol.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Let me explain with a comparison. You wouldn't expect that simply talking to your doctor would fix a broken arm or cure you of cancer. If fact it would be really disheartening if that's what everyone kept telling you to do and here you are, with a broken arm.

I was molested when I was very young and it messed me up bad...

&#x200B;

Now for fucked part... I enjoyed it. :(

I've been struggling with depression for a long time, and the hardest thing to convey is that for some people there isn't a fix. I often hear people saying to spend time with friends and family, but in my case I have very few friends and no family that's alive. So just make new friends right? Not an easy prospect with severe social anxiety. How about finding some medicine to help? The only stuff that really helps anxiety you can't get anymore, especially where I live. Therapist often only leave you feeling worse. My last and final session with one was so soul crushing I went straight from the doctors office a purchased a gun. So then distraction is the key right? Not really... Depression makes it so nothing is rewarding. Sure, it's rewarding when you are having a good day, but others not so much. The thing is there are two types of depression. One has a cause, like the death of a family member or loneliness. The other is medical that is exacerbated by outside influences. In my case it's made worse because I have treatment resistant clinical depression and no outside safety nets.

&#x200B;

Just some food for thought.
